    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e9d952b0_6fdad6e8",
        "filename": "java/dexpreopt_config.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 149,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-24T13:45:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Ok, so we expect a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of boot images, and visited is to avoid recomputing the same parts multiple times, right?\n\nWe expect the boot images to form a tree, not a DAG, because every boot image can only extend one boot image. The root of the tree is the primary boot image.\n\n\u003e Please add a comment clarifying that (with a plausible A - B - C example), and saying that we do not expect loops and we let them crash soong with a stackoverflow.\n\nDone.\n\n\u003e And TBH, I still prefer to set visited before the recursive call, as we are not expecting loops anyway. We would notice the failure anyway.\n\nI prefer surfacing errors in a fatal way rather than let the code misbehave silently.\n\n\u003e Do we have a test for DAG but not a tree of boot images?\n\nWe only expect a tree. The tests are in `java/dexpreopt_config_testing.go`. For any change to the boot image config, the tests must be updated with the expected calculation results. In the next CL, you can see that I updated the tests after add a boot image config for mainline BCP jars. It\u0027s not necessary to test non-existing cases because the configs are static.",
      "parentUuid": "78240728_27c15b54",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 149,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 149,
        "endChar": 23
      },
      "revId": "eb5bb9745986780bf11555d0b9664e2431bc8744",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "96bccadb_d9a4b27e",
        "filename": "java/dexpreopt_config.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 149,
      "author": {
        "id": 1550939
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-24T13:59:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We expect the boot images to form a tree\n\nThen why is `visited` needed at all (as you don\u0027t need it to perform DFS on a tree)? What is the scenario when part of the tree may already be visited? If there is such a scenario, please add it to the comment.",
      "parentUuid": "e9d952b0_6fdad6e8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 149,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 149,
        "endChar": 23
      },
      "revId": "eb5bb9745986780bf11555d0b9664e2431bc8744",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d765d161_d6dfe255",
        "filename": "java/dexpreopt_config.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 149,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884045
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-24T14:32:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Note that a boot image config only has a pointer to the parent, not to children. Therefore, we have to go in the reverse direction: we are going from the leaves to the root rather than from the root to the leaves.\n\nThe constraint we have is that we must finish the visit of the parent before we visit a child.\n\nThe `visited` map makes this possible: we use a for loop on line 134 to start from every node, go to the parent, visit it, or skip it if it\u0027s already visited, then go back to visit the child.\n\nHere\u0027s an example:\n\nThe tree is:\n\n```\n  B\n  |\n  A\n / \\\nC   D\n```\n\nThe sequence of the configs is `[A B C D]`.\n\nIteration 1:\n\nStart from A -\u003e Go to B -\u003e Visit B -\u003e Visit A\n\nIteration 2:\n\nStart from B (skipped)\n\nIteration 3:\n\nStart from C -\u003e Go to A (skipped) -\u003e Visit C\n\nIteration 4:\n\nStart from D -\u003e Go to A (skipped) -\u003e Visit D\n\nThe sequence above is just an example. With this algorithm, we can handle any sequence of the configs. Actually, the sequence of the configs is non-deterministic because it come from the map returned by `genBootImageConfigRaw`.\n\nIf we don\u0027t use this way, we\u0027ll have to find the root, find the children of every node, maintain this information somewhere, and then start the visit from the root. The use of the `visited` map can be avoided, but the code will be more complicated.",
      "parentUuid": "96bccadb_d9a4b27e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 149,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 149,
        "endChar": 23
      },
      "revId": "eb5bb9745986780bf11555d0b9664e2431bc8744",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
